From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 07:42:42 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] x87: FSIN, FCOS, FPTAN, F2XM1, FYL2X and FYL2XP1 follow an
 Intel core

The soft-float transcendental instructions called the cephes 128-bit
functions on the operand and rounded the result to 80 bits. An Intel
core (a Xeon Platinum 8581C decided every row) differs in these ways,
which the new cephes/X87Math.cpp reproduces for both the FEXCore
interpreter fallbacks and the gadget engine that links the library:

- FSIN, FCOS and FPTAN reduce a finite operand below 2^63 by pi/2 to 66
  bits, not by the full constant: the remainder is exact, and the
  quadrant picks the function (the tangent is -1/tan(r) in an odd
  quadrant). A magnitude below 2^-68 is returned as it is (sine and
  tangent) or as one (cosine). A sine, cosine or tangent that rounds to
  its argument or to one is stepped one place toward zero first, so the
  80-bit rounding sees an inexact result in the direction x86 reports
  (FPTAN of a remainder between 2^-68 and 2^-34 that is not a power of
  two falls short of it too).
- F2XM1 outside [-1, 1] returns the operand with PE, as x86 does not
  compute there; a tiny operand keeps its full precision through a
  series; zero and the infinities return their x86 values.
- FYL2X and the new FYL2XP1 handle zero, infinities, negative operands
  and NaNs as x86 does (a NaN takes precedence over a denormal), keep
  the precision of log2(1 + x) for a tiny x, and short the logarithm of
  a power of two below one by one place.
- PE is set for every computed result, exact or not.
- A pseudo-denormal operand converts as its significand with exponent 1.

The microcode's own rounding noise (about 9% of F2XM1, FYL2X and
FYL2XP1 results one place off, about 4% of FPTAN, under 2% of FSIN and
FCOS, and about 1% of results differing only in C1) is not a function of
the operand that can be reproduced and remains.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
